1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,280
Sweetheart, in December of nineteen sixty-eight,

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:05,603
Harold and I brought a little girl

3
00:00:05,603 --> 00:00:08,337
home from the county children's home for

4
00:00:08,337 --> 00:00:09,020
five days.

5
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:10,861
Just for Christmas.

6
00:00:11,041 --> 00:00:12,910
Her name was Marie.

7
00:00:13,090 --> 00:00:14,628
She was eight years old,

8
00:00:14,628 --> 00:00:16,743
and she had never had a Christmas

9
00:00:16,743 --> 00:00:17,769
in a real house.

10
00:00:17,949 --> 00:00:19,569
On the twenty-sixth,

11
00:00:19,569 --> 00:00:21,594
we had to drive her back.

12
00:00:21,774 --> 00:00:24,100
She screamed in the back seat the

13
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:24,804
whole way.

14
00:00:24,984 --> 00:00:26,579
And fifty years later,

15
00:00:26,579 --> 00:00:28,247
she knocked on my door.

16
00:00:28,597 --> 00:00:30,999
Tonight I'm going to tell you what

17
00:00:30,999 --> 00:00:33,260
happened during those five days,

18
00:00:33,260 --> 00:00:35,027
why we couldn't keep her,

19
00:00:35,027 --> 00:00:37,218
and what she was holding in her

20
00:00:37,218 --> 00:00:39,267
arms when I opened my door in

21
00:00:39,267 --> 00:00:40,822
two thousand eighteen.

22
00:00:41,002 --> 00:00:41,982
Stay with me,

23
00:00:41,982 --> 00:00:45,451
honey, because it's something Harold made with

24
00:00:45,451 --> 00:00:46,507
his own hands,

25
00:00:46,507 --> 00:00:48,769
and I thought it had been lost

26
00:00:48,769 --> 00:00:49,373
forever.

27
00:00:49,722 --> 00:00:52,517
Our pastor announced it from the pulpit

28
00:00:52,517 --> 00:00:53,879
in November, honey.

29
00:00:54,059 --> 00:00:57,359
The county children's home had thirty-one children,

30
00:00:57,359 --> 00:00:59,495
and the state could only afford a

31
00:00:59,495 --> 00:01:01,954
small party for them on Christmas Day.

32
00:01:02,134 --> 00:01:04,528
So the church was asking families to

33
00:01:04,528 --> 00:01:06,789
take one child home for Christmas.

34
00:01:06,969 --> 00:01:08,920
Just five days.

35
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:11,654
The twenty-second to the twenty-sixth.

36
00:01:11,834 --> 00:01:13,955
Harold looked at me in the pew,

37
00:01:13,955 --> 00:01:15,323
and I looked at him,

38
00:01:15,323 --> 00:01:17,376
and we didn't even have to say

39
00:01:17,376 --> 00:01:17,581
it.

40
00:01:17,930 --> 00:01:19,681
We had Susan by then,

41
00:01:19,681 --> 00:01:21,349
honey, she was four,

42
00:01:21,349 --> 00:01:23,434
and Mark was just a baby.

43
00:01:23,614 --> 00:01:25,088
We didn't have much.

44
00:01:25,268 --> 00:01:27,648
Harold was working at the plant and

45
00:01:27,648 --> 00:01:29,960
we were living in our first little

46
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:31,388
house on Maple Drive,

47
00:01:31,388 --> 00:01:33,836
with one bathroom and a furnace that

48
00:01:33,836 --> 00:01:35,264
groaned like a ghost.

49
00:01:35,444 --> 00:01:37,196
But we had a spare cot,

50
00:01:37,196 --> 00:01:38,567
and we had enough.

51
00:01:38,747 --> 00:01:39,815
And I thought,

52
00:01:39,815 --> 00:01:42,333
if a child has to spend Christmas

53
00:01:42,333 --> 00:01:43,706
in an institution,

54
00:01:43,706 --> 00:01:45,690
then we've all failed her.

55
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:48,498
The children's home was a big brick

56
00:01:48,498 --> 00:01:50,534
building on the edge of town,

57
00:01:50,534 --> 00:01:53,342
with a chain-link fence and a playground

58
00:01:53,342 --> 00:01:54,395
with no swings,

59
00:01:54,395 --> 00:01:56,501
because the chains had rusted.

60
00:01:56,681 --> 00:01:59,065
The matron brought Marie out to us

61
00:01:59,065 --> 00:02:00,327
in the front hall.

62
00:02:00,507 --> 00:02:02,567
She had a little paper bag with

63
00:02:02,567 --> 00:02:04,361
everything she owned in it.

64
00:02:04,541 --> 00:02:06,768
A nightgown, a toothbrush,

65
00:02:06,768 --> 00:02:08,995
a comb, and a picture of a

66
00:02:08,995 --> 00:02:11,565
woman, torn out of a magazine.

67
00:02:11,745 --> 00:02:13,740
In a minute I'll tell you who

68
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:15,391
she said that woman was.

69
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:17,606
She was small for eight,

70
00:02:17,606 --> 00:02:20,094
honey, with dark braids and big,

71
00:02:20,094 --> 00:02:21,104
careful eyes.

72
00:02:21,284 --> 00:02:22,805
She didn't smile.

73
00:02:22,985 --> 00:02:25,110
She didn't talk in the car.

74
00:02:25,290 --> 00:02:26,499
When we got home,

75
00:02:26,499 --> 00:02:28,703
she stood in the doorway of our

76
00:02:28,703 --> 00:02:31,477
living room and looked at the Christmas

77
00:02:31,477 --> 00:02:33,325
tree for a very long time,

78
00:02:33,325 --> 00:02:34,392
without moving.

79
00:02:34,572 --> 00:02:35,826
Then she said,

80
00:02:35,826 --> 00:02:38,334
very quietly, is that yours?

81
00:02:38,514 --> 00:02:40,967
Like she couldn't believe a family could

82
00:02:40,967 --> 00:02:42,194
have their own tree.

83
00:02:42,543 --> 00:02:44,811
That night, when I tucked her in

84
00:02:44,811 --> 00:02:46,725
on the cot in Susan's room,

85
00:02:46,725 --> 00:02:49,134
I asked her about the picture from

86
00:02:49,134 --> 00:02:50,055
the magazine.

87
00:02:50,235 --> 00:02:52,665
It was a lady in an advertisement

88
00:02:52,665 --> 00:02:53,991
for soap, smiling,

89
00:02:53,991 --> 00:02:55,390
with a little girl.

90
00:02:55,570 --> 00:02:56,897
And Marie said,

91
00:02:56,897 --> 00:02:58,136
that's my mom.

92
00:02:58,316 --> 00:02:59,872
I knew she wasn't,

93
00:02:59,872 --> 00:03:00,824
sweetheart.

94
00:03:01,004 --> 00:03:03,812
Marie had been left at the hospital

95
00:03:03,812 --> 00:03:04,614
as a baby.

96
00:03:04,794 --> 00:03:07,084
She'd been in the home her whole

97
00:03:07,084 --> 00:03:07,441
life.

98
00:03:07,621 --> 00:03:10,088
She'd just picked a lady with a

99
00:03:10,088 --> 00:03:10,884
kind face.

100
00:03:11,064 --> 00:03:13,490
I went in the hall and cried.

101
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:15,110
The next morning,

102
00:03:15,110 --> 00:03:17,573
honey, I found her sitting on the

103
00:03:17,573 --> 00:03:18,767
edge of the cot,

104
00:03:18,767 --> 00:03:21,455
fully dressed, with her shoes on and

105
00:03:21,455 --> 00:03:23,321
her paper bag in her lap,

106
00:03:23,321 --> 00:03:24,440
at six o'clock.

107
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:26,897
I asked her why she was up

108
00:03:26,897 --> 00:03:27,685
so early.

109
00:03:27,865 --> 00:03:29,325
She said, at the home,

110
00:03:29,325 --> 00:03:31,648
if you're not ready when they call,

111
00:03:31,648 --> 00:03:33,241
you don't get breakfast.

112
00:03:33,421 --> 00:03:35,497
I sat down next to her and

113
00:03:35,497 --> 00:03:38,052
said, sweetheart, in this house,

114
00:03:38,052 --> 00:03:39,969
breakfast waits for you.

115
00:03:40,149 --> 00:03:41,821
She didn't believe me.

116
00:03:42,001 --> 00:03:43,957
I'll tell you how long it took

117
00:03:43,957 --> 00:03:45,913
her to believe me in a minute.

118
00:03:46,263 --> 00:03:49,403
If Grandma's story is touching your heart,

119
00:03:49,403 --> 00:03:51,420
tap the like button for me,

120
00:03:51,420 --> 00:03:52,243
sweetheart.

121
00:03:52,423 --> 00:03:55,418
It helps more people find these stories.

122
00:03:55,598 --> 00:03:56,875
Those five days,

123
00:03:56,875 --> 00:03:59,589
honey, we tried to give that child

124
00:03:59,589 --> 00:04:01,983
a whole lifetime of Christmas.

125
00:04:02,163 --> 00:04:03,177
We made cookies,

126
00:04:03,177 --> 00:04:05,013
and she ate the dough off the

127
00:04:05,013 --> 00:04:07,292
spoon, and she laughed for the first

128
00:04:07,292 --> 00:04:07,609
time.

129
00:04:07,789 --> 00:04:10,041
We went caroling with the church.

130
00:04:10,221 --> 00:04:13,483
Susan followed her everywhere like a puppy.

131
00:04:13,663 --> 00:04:16,047
And Marie held Mark like he was

132
00:04:16,047 --> 00:04:17,123
made of glass.

133
00:04:17,474 --> 00:04:19,203
But Harold, honey.

134
00:04:19,383 --> 00:04:21,918
Harold went down to the basement every

135
00:04:21,918 --> 00:04:23,185
night after supper,

136
00:04:23,185 --> 00:04:25,253
and he wouldn't tell me what he

137
00:04:25,253 --> 00:04:25,920
was doing.

138
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:29,490
I heard sawing and hammering until midnight.

139
00:04:29,670 --> 00:04:31,112
On the twenty-third,

140
00:04:31,112 --> 00:04:32,410
the twenty-fourth.

141
00:04:32,590 --> 00:04:34,903
He came to bed with sawdust in

142
00:04:34,903 --> 00:04:35,597
his hair.

143
00:04:35,777 --> 00:04:37,785
And on Christmas morning,

144
00:04:37,785 --> 00:04:38,749
I found out.

145
00:04:38,929 --> 00:04:40,972
He'd built her a dollhouse.

146
00:04:41,152 --> 00:04:43,685
Two stories, with a front porch,

147
00:04:43,685 --> 00:04:46,377
painted white with green shutters.

148
00:04:46,727 --> 00:04:48,441
And it was our house,

149
00:04:48,441 --> 00:04:49,339
sweetheart.

150
00:04:49,519 --> 00:04:52,145
Our little house on Maple Drive,

151
00:04:52,145 --> 00:04:55,099
exactly, down to the front porch and

152
00:04:55,099 --> 00:04:56,740
the crooked mailbox.

153
00:04:56,920 --> 00:04:59,323
He'd painted the number on the door.

154
00:04:59,503 --> 00:05:02,792
And inside, I'd sewn four little cloth

155
00:05:02,792 --> 00:05:03,312
dolls.

156
00:05:03,492 --> 00:05:05,377
A mother, a father,

157
00:05:05,377 --> 00:05:06,766
a little girl,

158
00:05:06,766 --> 00:05:07,857
and a baby.

159
00:05:08,037 --> 00:05:10,558
And Harold had written on the bottom,

160
00:05:10,558 --> 00:05:11,988
in pencil, for Marie,

161
00:05:11,988 --> 00:05:14,237
who will always have a home here.

162
00:05:14,417 --> 00:05:15,426
Stay with me,

163
00:05:15,426 --> 00:05:18,610
honey, because that's the part I'll never

164
00:05:18,610 --> 00:05:19,153
forget.

165
00:05:19,504 --> 00:05:21,994
It took her until the third morning,

166
00:05:21,994 --> 00:05:22,754
sweetheart.

167
00:05:22,934 --> 00:05:24,543
On the twenty-fourth,

168
00:05:24,543 --> 00:05:26,842
she slept until eight o'clock,

169
00:05:26,842 --> 00:05:29,217
and she came down the stairs in

170
00:05:29,217 --> 00:05:31,668
her nightgown, rubbing her eyes,

171
00:05:31,668 --> 00:05:34,273
and she stopped on the bottom step

172
00:05:34,273 --> 00:05:36,112
and looked at the table.

173
00:05:36,292 --> 00:05:37,794
Her plate was there,

174
00:05:37,794 --> 00:05:40,273
with pancakes under a dish towel,

175
00:05:40,273 --> 00:05:41,249
keeping warm.

176
00:05:41,429 --> 00:05:43,205
And she just stood there.

177
00:05:43,385 --> 00:05:45,531
Then she walked over and lifted the

178
00:05:45,531 --> 00:05:47,370
towel, and she looked up at me

179
00:05:47,370 --> 00:05:49,086
like I'd done a magic trick.

180
00:05:49,436 --> 00:05:52,112
Marie sat in front of that dollhouse

181
00:05:52,112 --> 00:05:53,673
all of Christmas Day.

182
00:05:53,853 --> 00:05:56,187
She didn't play with anything else.

183
00:05:56,367 --> 00:05:59,262
She moved the four little dolls from

184
00:05:59,262 --> 00:06:00,307
room to room,

185
00:06:00,307 --> 00:06:01,352
all day long,

186
00:06:01,352 --> 00:06:02,880
whispering to them.

187
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:04,377
And that night,

188
00:06:04,377 --> 00:06:05,519
she asked me,

189
00:06:05,519 --> 00:06:06,485
can I stay?

190
00:06:06,665 --> 00:06:09,016
And I didn't know what to say,

191
00:06:09,016 --> 00:06:09,486
honey.

192
00:06:09,666 --> 00:06:12,476
Because the rules said five days.

193
00:06:12,656 --> 00:06:15,528
And the rules said families like ours,

194
00:06:15,528 --> 00:06:17,871
with a baby and a new mortgage,

195
00:06:17,871 --> 00:06:20,214
weren't approved for fostering.

196
00:06:20,564 --> 00:06:22,195
On the twenty-sixth,

197
00:06:22,195 --> 00:06:23,663
we drove her back.

198
00:06:23,843 --> 00:06:26,134
She held that dollhouse on her lap

199
00:06:26,134 --> 00:06:27,279
in the back seat,

200
00:06:27,279 --> 00:06:29,636
and when she realized where we were

201
00:06:29,636 --> 00:06:30,984
going, she screamed.

202
00:06:31,164 --> 00:06:33,137
She screamed the whole way.

203
00:06:33,317 --> 00:06:35,397
And at the children's home,

204
00:06:35,397 --> 00:06:36,630
the matron said,

205
00:06:36,630 --> 00:06:38,170
she can't keep that.

206
00:06:38,350 --> 00:06:40,846
There's no room for private toys.

207
00:06:41,026 --> 00:06:43,592
It'll be shared with the others.

208
00:06:43,772 --> 00:06:46,537
And Marie held onto that dollhouse with

209
00:06:46,537 --> 00:06:49,232
both arms until they pried her fingers

210
00:06:49,232 --> 00:06:49,728
off it.

211
00:06:50,078 --> 00:06:52,612
Harold didn't say a word the whole

212
00:06:52,612 --> 00:06:54,327
drive home, sweetheart.

213
00:06:54,507 --> 00:06:57,270
He just gripped the steering wheel.

214
00:06:57,450 --> 00:06:59,784
And when we got in the driveway,

215
00:06:59,784 --> 00:07:02,118
he sat there with the engine off

216
00:07:02,118 --> 00:07:03,285
for a long time,

217
00:07:03,285 --> 00:07:05,254
and then he put his head on

218
00:07:05,254 --> 00:07:05,983
the wheel.

219
00:07:06,163 --> 00:07:08,590
My Harold, who never cried.

220
00:07:08,770 --> 00:07:10,022
He said, Ruthie,

221
00:07:10,022 --> 00:07:11,823
I want to get her back.

222
00:07:12,003 --> 00:07:13,417
And we tried,

223
00:07:13,417 --> 00:07:14,070
honey.

224
00:07:14,250 --> 00:07:15,898
We tried so hard.

225
00:07:16,078 --> 00:07:18,195
In a minute I'll tell you what

226
00:07:18,195 --> 00:07:18,830
happened.

227
00:07:19,180 --> 00:07:21,622
Harold went to the county office the

228
00:07:21,622 --> 00:07:22,639
very next week.

229
00:07:22,819 --> 00:07:24,828
He filled out every form.

230
00:07:25,008 --> 00:07:27,184
We had a social worker come to

231
00:07:27,184 --> 00:07:29,577
the house and inspect everything,

232
00:07:29,577 --> 00:07:31,463
the bedrooms, the kitchen,

233
00:07:31,463 --> 00:07:32,334
the furnace.

234
00:07:32,514 --> 00:07:34,824
Our pastor wrote a letter.

235
00:07:35,004 --> 00:07:35,985
But in March,

236
00:07:35,985 --> 00:07:37,570
the letter came back.

237
00:07:37,750 --> 00:07:40,340
Marie had been placed with a family

238
00:07:40,340 --> 00:07:43,004
in Michigan, who were going to adopt

239
00:07:43,004 --> 00:07:43,300
her.

240
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:45,987
The county wouldn't tell us their name.

241
00:07:46,167 --> 00:07:47,593
It was the rules,

242
00:07:47,593 --> 00:07:48,431
back then.

243
00:07:48,782 --> 00:07:51,671
I told myself it was good news,

244
00:07:51,671 --> 00:07:52,230
honey.

245
00:07:52,410 --> 00:07:53,954
A real family.

246
00:07:54,134 --> 00:07:55,585
A forever home.

247
00:07:55,765 --> 00:07:58,189
But Harold never got over it,

248
00:07:58,189 --> 00:07:59,109
not really.

249
00:07:59,289 --> 00:08:01,535
Every Christmas, for forty years,

250
00:08:01,535 --> 00:08:03,509
he'd go quiet for a minute on

251
00:08:03,509 --> 00:08:05,960
Christmas morning, and I knew he was

252
00:08:05,960 --> 00:08:07,253
thinking about her.

253
00:08:07,433 --> 00:08:10,960
About that dollhouse with somebody else's children

254
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:12,089
playing with it.

255
00:08:12,269 --> 00:08:14,653
He passed in two thousand eight,

256
00:08:14,653 --> 00:08:15,845
still wondering.

257
00:08:16,025 --> 00:08:18,521
That's the part that hurts the most.

258
00:08:18,871 --> 00:08:20,436
So far, the five days,

259
00:08:20,436 --> 00:08:21,432
the soap lady,

260
00:08:21,432 --> 00:08:24,208
the dollhouse, and the screaming in the

261
00:08:24,208 --> 00:08:24,919
back seat.

262
00:08:25,099 --> 00:08:27,637
Ten years after Harold passed,

263
00:08:27,637 --> 00:08:30,428
on the twenty-second of December,

264
00:08:30,428 --> 00:08:32,289
two thousand eighteen,

265
00:08:32,289 --> 00:08:34,911
exactly fifty years to the day,

266
00:08:34,911 --> 00:08:36,349
my doorbell rang.

267
00:08:36,529 --> 00:08:37,957
I was seventy-four.

268
00:08:38,137 --> 00:08:40,628
And standing on my porch was a

269
00:08:40,628 --> 00:08:42,870
woman of about fifty-eight,

270
00:08:42,870 --> 00:08:45,111
with gray in her dark hair,

271
00:08:45,111 --> 00:08:46,938
and big, careful eyes.

272
00:08:47,118 --> 00:08:48,336
And in her arms,

273
00:08:48,336 --> 00:08:49,783
wrapped in a quilt,

274
00:08:49,783 --> 00:08:52,981
was a white dollhouse with green shutters.

275
00:08:53,331 --> 00:08:54,910
I couldn't breathe,

276
00:08:54,910 --> 00:08:55,409
honey.

277
00:08:55,589 --> 00:08:58,015
I put my hand on the doorframe.

278
00:08:58,195 --> 00:08:59,722
And she said,

279
00:08:59,722 --> 00:09:00,192
Mrs.

280
00:09:00,372 --> 00:09:01,127
Ruth?

281
00:09:01,307 --> 00:09:03,536
I don't know if you remember me.

282
00:09:03,716 --> 00:09:05,136
And I said,

283
00:09:05,136 --> 00:09:05,910
Marie.

284
00:09:06,090 --> 00:09:07,449
Oh, Marie.

285
00:09:07,629 --> 00:09:09,556
Of course I remember you.

286
00:09:09,736 --> 00:09:10,800
And she said,

287
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:12,929
I think this belongs here.

288
00:09:13,109 --> 00:09:15,722
And she turned the dollhouse around,

289
00:09:15,722 --> 00:09:18,191
and there was the crooked mailbox,

290
00:09:18,191 --> 00:09:20,586
and our house number on the front

291
00:09:20,586 --> 00:09:22,692
door, faded, but still there,

292
00:09:22,692 --> 00:09:23,999
in Harold's paint.

293
00:09:24,349 --> 00:09:26,814
She told me at my kitchen table,

294
00:09:26,814 --> 00:09:30,049
sweetheart, with the dollhouse between us.

295
00:09:30,229 --> 00:09:32,950
When the children's home closed in nineteen

296
00:09:32,950 --> 00:09:35,860
seventy-four, a woman who'd worked there found

297
00:09:35,860 --> 00:09:37,948
that dollhouse in a storage room,

298
00:09:37,948 --> 00:09:40,353
with the pencil writing on the bottom.

299
00:09:40,533 --> 00:09:42,692
She kept it for forty years.

300
00:09:42,872 --> 00:09:44,461
And when she was dying,

301
00:09:44,461 --> 00:09:47,085
her daughter found Marie's name on the

302
00:09:47,085 --> 00:09:49,710
bottom and tracked her down through an

303
00:09:49,710 --> 00:09:50,953
adoption registry.

304
00:09:51,133 --> 00:09:53,049
It had taken a year.

305
00:09:53,399 --> 00:09:56,808
Marie's adoptive family in Michigan were good

306
00:09:56,808 --> 00:09:57,868
people, honey.

307
00:09:58,048 --> 00:09:59,476
She had a good life.

308
00:09:59,656 --> 00:10:01,328
She became a teacher,

309
00:10:01,328 --> 00:10:03,000
and then a principal.

310
00:10:03,180 --> 00:10:06,361
She has three children and five grandchildren.

311
00:10:06,541 --> 00:10:08,885
But she told me she'd dreamed about

312
00:10:08,885 --> 00:10:10,558
our house her whole life.

313
00:10:10,738 --> 00:10:11,888
The cookies.

314
00:10:12,068 --> 00:10:13,159
The caroling.

315
00:10:13,339 --> 00:10:15,928
A man with sawdust in his hair.

316
00:10:16,108 --> 00:10:18,036
She said, for five days,

317
00:10:18,036 --> 00:10:20,204
I knew what it felt like to

318
00:10:20,204 --> 00:10:21,007
be wanted.

319
00:10:21,187 --> 00:10:23,979
Everything good in my life grew out

320
00:10:23,979 --> 00:10:25,495
of those five days.

321
00:10:25,845 --> 00:10:27,868
And then she said,

322
00:10:27,868 --> 00:10:28,317
Mrs.

323
00:10:28,497 --> 00:10:30,831
Ruth, is Harold here?

324
00:10:31,011 --> 00:10:32,509
I'd like to thank him.

325
00:10:32,689 --> 00:10:34,523
And I had to tell her.

326
00:10:34,703 --> 00:10:35,781
And she cried,

327
00:10:35,781 --> 00:10:38,244
honey, for a man she'd known for

328
00:10:38,244 --> 00:10:40,322
five days, fifty years ago.

329
00:10:40,502 --> 00:10:42,012
So the next morning,

330
00:10:42,012 --> 00:10:44,879
we drove out to the cemetery together,

331
00:10:44,879 --> 00:10:45,785
in the snow.

332
00:10:45,965 --> 00:10:48,347
And Marie knelt down by his stone,

333
00:10:48,347 --> 00:10:50,520
and she put one of those little

334
00:10:50,520 --> 00:10:51,851
cloth dolls on top.

335
00:10:52,031 --> 00:10:52,995
The father.

336
00:10:53,175 --> 00:10:55,309
She'd kept it in her pocket for

337
00:10:55,309 --> 00:10:56,135
fifty years.

338
00:10:56,486 --> 00:10:58,715
Marie comes every Christmas now,

339
00:10:58,715 --> 00:11:01,153
sweetheart, with her grandchildren.

340
00:11:01,333 --> 00:11:03,591
The dollhouse sits on a table in

341
00:11:03,591 --> 00:11:04,649
my living room,

342
00:11:04,649 --> 00:11:06,695
right in front of the window,

343
00:11:06,695 --> 00:11:08,601
where Harold would want it.

344
00:11:08,781 --> 00:11:09,923
And every year,

345
00:11:09,923 --> 00:11:13,274
her grandchildren move the little dolls from

346
00:11:13,274 --> 00:11:14,265
room to room,

347
00:11:14,265 --> 00:11:15,712
whispering to them.

348
00:11:15,892 --> 00:11:16,997
And every year,

349
00:11:16,997 --> 00:11:19,133
I tell them about the man who

350
00:11:19,133 --> 00:11:20,975
built it in the basement,

351
00:11:20,975 --> 00:11:21,933
in five days,

352
00:11:21,933 --> 00:11:23,775
with sawdust in his hair.

353
00:11:24,125 --> 00:11:26,959
Here's what I want you to remember,

354
00:11:26,959 --> 00:11:27,445
honey.

355
00:11:27,625 --> 00:11:31,008
Five days of love can last fifty

356
00:11:31,008 --> 00:11:31,642
years.

357
00:11:31,822 --> 00:11:33,988
You never know what a child will

358
00:11:33,988 --> 00:11:36,019
carry with her for the rest of

359
00:11:36,019 --> 00:11:36,629
her life.

360
00:11:36,809 --> 00:11:38,654
Tell me in the comments,

361
00:11:38,654 --> 00:11:41,806
sweetheart, who made you feel wanted when

362
00:11:41,806 --> 00:11:43,113
you were a child?

363
00:11:43,293 --> 00:11:45,473
There are more of my family's stories

364
00:11:45,473 --> 00:11:47,536
in the playlist right there on your

365
00:11:47,536 --> 00:11:47,948
screen.

366
00:11:48,128 --> 00:11:48,964
Good night.

367
00:11:49,144 --> 00:11:50,607
Merry Christmas.
